  "clock": {
    "notes": "The clock accessors `time_origin` and `timestamp_to_seconds` (API_SPEC §5.8), in the bindings that carry them. Each case gives a document's `domain` as raw JSON (null: the document has none). A binding calls both accessors on that raw value, and, when `typed` is true, on the typed domain built from it and on a document built in memory that carries it, both built in memory rather than loaded; every form MUST give the same answer. `typed` is false for a domain whose shape only raw JSON can hold (a `null` origin field, a `temporal` that is not an object). `origin` is what `time_origin` returns; each `timestamps` entry is what `timestamp_to_seconds` returns for it. A value is binary64 seconds, compared EXACTLY; a refusal is its code, and every refusal is one of `timestamp_malformed`, `timestamp_no_calendar_day` and `time_origin_invalid`, checked in that order. Values were computed from the esm-spec §2.2.7 arithmetic, not by a binding.",
    "bindings_required": [
      "julia",
      "python",
      "rust"
    ],
    "fields": {
      "domain": "The document's `domain`, as raw JSON; null when the document has none.",
      "typed": "Whether the typed domain can hold this one, so that a binding also runs the case on the typed domain and on a document built in memory that carries it.",
      "origin": "`{\"seconds\": x}`: `time_origin` returns x. `{\"error_code\": c}`: it raises c.",
      "timestamps": "Each `{timestamp, seconds}` or `{timestamp, error_code}`: what `timestamp_to_seconds` returns or raises. A non-string `timestamp` is `timestamp_malformed`; a binding whose signature admits only a string (Rust) skips it."
    },
